.he "mail (1)                     9/30/26                     mail (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
mail
- send and receive mail
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
mail [ user ... ]
.sp
.bd
mail [ -pqr ] [ -f file ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Mail carries letters between the users of a machine.  A letter is
plain text; mail puts a line at its head saying who it is from and
when, and that header is where one letter ends and the next begins.
.sp
With one or more user names, mail is
.ul
sending
something.  The letter is typed on the standard input and ends at a
line holding a single period, or at end of file.  Each name is looked
up in /etc/passwd, and the letter is appended to that user's mailbox,
/usr/spool/mail/<name>, which is created if it is not there and made
over to its owner.  A name that is not in /etc/passwd is reported; if
any of the names failed, the whole letter is written to dead.letter
in the current directory as well, so that nothing typed is lost.
.sp
A line of the letter that begins with
.bd
From
is given a
.bd
>
in front of it, so that a letter cannot forge the header that begins
another one.
.sp
With no names, or with an option first, mail is
.ul
reading
the mailbox instead.  The letters are printed one at a time, newest
first, and mail prompts after each one for a command.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
COMMANDS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
<newline>, n, +	Go on to the next letter, leaving this one in the mailbox.

.ti -10
p	Print this letter again.

.ti -10
-	Go back to the previous letter.  ^ does the same.

.ti -10
d	Delete this letter and go on to the next.  dq deletes it and then quits.

.ti -10
s [file]	Save this letter, header and all, at the end of file, and delete it from the mailbox.  With no file it is saved in mbox in the current directory.  y is a synonym for s.

.ti -10
w [file]	The same, but without the header.

.ti -10
m user	Forward this letter to user, who need not be the user reading it.  A name with a ! in it is an address on another machine, handed to uucp; a plain name is delivered by running mail again with the letter on its standard input.

.ti -10
! cmd	Run cmd with the shell.

.ti -10
x	Quit without changing the mailbox: the letters read are not deleted.

.ti -10
q	Quit, keeping the deletions.

.ti -10
?	Print this list.

.in -10
.sp
On quitting, mail rewrites the mailbox without the letters that were
deleted.  If new mail arrived while the mailbox was being read it is
kept, and a note is printed.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-p	Print every letter without ever prompting.  Nothing is deleted, which is what makes it useful in a script.

.ti -10
-q	Do not return to the command prompt when interrupted: a signal ends mail instead.  This is for a session with nobody at the keyboard to answer the prompt.

.ti -10
-r	Print the letters in the order they arrived, oldest first, instead of newest first.

.ti -10
-f file	Work on file instead of the user's own mailbox.  The name is a separate argument.  Deletions are written back to that file.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
A mailbox is locked by setting its execute bit for the duration, and a
mailbox whose lock is less than a minute old is refused with a
complaint.  There is no lock file and no flock call; the mode bit is
the whole of it, and the minute is what makes a lock left behind by a
program that was killed recoverable.
.sp
Micronix has no sendmail and no mailer of any kind, so mail delivers
by appending to the spool file itself, as v7 did and as the later
Berkeley mail stopped doing.  A
.bd
m
command with a
.bd
!
in the address would need uucp; there is none on this system, and the
attempt fails at the shell.
.sp
Three differences from the v7 source are worth knowing about, because
they are visible from outside.  The name on the header is the login
name of the real user I.D., since this libc has no getlogin and there
is nothing else to ask.  The remote-from line names this machine by a
constant, since micronix keeps no system name.  And a forward runs
the shell to redirect the letter into a second copy of mail, because
there is no popen here to hand a pipe to a command.
.sp
The mail that shipped on the 1982 distribution disk is a smaller and
different program: it asks after each letter whether to keep it, and
answers yes or no.  This is the v7 mail, which asks with a lettered
command instead, and the two do not read the same way.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/usr/spool/mail/<user>	- a user's mailbox
.sp
/etc/passwd	- where a user's login name is looked up
.sp
mbox	- where s and w save a letter by default
.sp
dead.letter	- a letter that could not be sent
.sp
/tmp/maXXXXXX	- the copy of the mailbox being read
.sp
/tmp/mbXXXXXX	- a letter being forwarded
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
login (1), who (1), write (1)
